"A dry bean looks like a small stone. You can keep it in a jar for a year, and nothing will happen. Then you put it on wet cotton, and in a few days it has a root. How did it know that the time had come?": "84dc74522b9a",
"A dry bean looks like a small stone. You can keep it in a jar for a year, and nothing will happen. Then you put it on wet cotton, and in a few days it has a root. How did it know that the time had come? A seed has three parts. On the outside is a tough coat. Inside is a very small plant, already formed, with the start of a root and the start of a shoot. The rest is stored food. In a bean, the food fills the two thick halves that you can pull apart with your fingers. The little plant is alive, but it is asleep. It will stay asleep until three things are right: water, warmth, and air. Some kinds of seed need light or darkness too. Waiting keeps a seed from starting to grow in a dry month or in the middle of winter. When the time is right, the seed soaks up water and swells until its coat splits. The root comes out first and grows down. Then the shoot grows up. It does not matter which way up the seed is lying. A plant can feel the pull of gravity, so its root grows toward the pull and its shoot grows away from it. Until its leaves open, the young plant cannot make food. It lives on what is packed in the seed, and it needs oxygen from the air to use that food. This is a race. The shoot has to reach the light before the packed lunch is gone. Bean sprouts show what happens without light. They are grown in the shade, so the plant keeps stretching upward, long and pale, and it does not turn green. In the light, the same plant would stay short, open green leaves, and begin to feed itself.": "a9aaa5cfd4ae",
